WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.133
-Our next guest is
an Emmy Award-winning

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:03.601
and Tony-nominated actor

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:06.667
you know from his work
in "Bridesmaids," "Moone Boy,"

00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:07.868
and "The IT Crowd."

00:00:07.868 --> 00:00:09.267
He stars in
"The Big Door Prize,"

00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:11.167
which premieres tomorrow
on Apple TV+.

00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:12.834
Let's take a look.
-Believe it or not,

00:00:12.834 --> 00:00:14.434
for the two bucks
you saved on coffee,

00:00:14.434 --> 00:00:18.667
that thing will tell you
your life's potential.

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:20.534
-Is that right? [ Chuckles ]

00:00:20.534 --> 00:00:22.801
Where'd you get it?
-No idea.

00:00:22.801 --> 00:00:25.701
Jacob was manning the store
when it arrived on Friday,

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:28.400
but a lot of people
used it over the weekend

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.734
and, uh, seems to be good
for business,

00:00:30.734 --> 00:00:33.267
so I'm not asking questions.

00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:35.100
-[ Chuckles ]

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.734
-Please welcome back to the show
Chris O'Dowd, everybody!

00:00:37.734 --> 00:00:40.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.634 --> 00:00:46.267
♪♪

00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:51.934
♪♪

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:53.434
Welcome back! It's been a while.

00:00:53.434 --> 00:00:55.601
-I know. It's been
like five years or something.

00:00:55.601 --> 00:00:57.467
-Five years.
I'm thrilled to have you back.

00:00:57.467 --> 00:01:00.133
This is a wonderful show
that's based on a book

00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:01.133
of the same name.

00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:02.534
A machine,
as we saw in the clip,

00:01:02.534 --> 00:01:03.934
arrives in a small town.

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:05.067
Lay out the premise
for us a little bit.

00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:06.934
-That's it.
Like, I play a...

00:01:06.934 --> 00:01:08.133
what do you call it?

00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:11.033
A high school history teacher
called Dusty.

00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:13.167
Very settled, very happy
kind of implant

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:14.434
from Ireland, maybe 20 years.

00:01:14.434 --> 00:01:16.000
But now this chapter
in his life,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.167
he's married, he's got a kid.

00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:19.100
He thinks he has it sorted.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.868
This machine arrives
in a local kind of grocery store

00:01:22.868 --> 00:01:24.234
with the promise
of telling everybody

00:01:24.234 --> 00:01:26.400
what their true life
potential is.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.868
So, you kind of sit
into the machine

00:01:28.868 --> 00:01:32.734
and it takes your fingerprints
and your Social Security number,

00:01:32.734 --> 00:01:34.267
at which point I'm out, right?
I'm out.

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:37.267
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-But this idiot...

00:01:37.267 --> 00:01:40.033
And then it takes
a few more details about you,

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.100
and everybody
in the course of the story

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.100
and every -- all of
the inhabitants of this town

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.434
start getting these cards with,

00:01:45.434 --> 00:01:50.267
like, whatever --
karate teacher or whatnot --

00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:53.367
and it starts changing
the course of their lives.

00:01:53.367 --> 00:01:56.501
Like, the school principal gets
"biker" and she gets a bike.

00:01:56.501 --> 00:02:00.200
And so it's this kind of idea
that the collective delusion

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.734
that if you want the oracle
to give you information,

00:02:03.734 --> 00:02:06.033
then it will
and you can follow it as much

00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:08.267
or as little as you want to,

00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:12.234
like a football team
or a religion or -- or whatnot.

00:02:12.234 --> 00:02:13.801
And so we see
all of these people.

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:15.300
This catalyst is kind of used

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.667
for everybody
to change their lives.

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:20.501
-And were you drawn
to the mysticism of this idea?

00:02:20.501 --> 00:02:22.000
-I've been here in America
for a while

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.868
and I see people chasing
a lot of pretty crazy ideas.

00:02:24.868 --> 00:02:26.701
-[ Chuckles ] Yeah.

00:02:26.701 --> 00:02:31.000
-Just because
somebody says it might be fun

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.234
and then
there's an insurrection.

00:02:33.234 --> 00:02:34.567
So I...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:34.567 --> 00:02:36.801
So I did...

00:02:36.801 --> 00:02:39.200
Which I'm sure was
its own fun on the day.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.234
-I think on the day.

00:02:40.234 --> 00:02:42.133
We do forget that they were all

00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:45.200
with a lot of like-minded people
having a grand old time.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.133
-And I suppose that doesn't
happen in this town yet.

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:49.567
But essentially what does happen

00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:51.267
is that people change
the relationships.

00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:52.901
People feel different
about themselves.

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:55.434
And it does offer the premise
that even though the book

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:57.067
was written
before the pandemic,

00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:59.734
it's got a reset kind of feel to
it where it's like if you're --

00:02:59.734 --> 00:03:01.634
if you think you're not
doing life right, sure,

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:04.567
just give it another whirl.
-[ Chuckles ] Did you --

00:03:04.567 --> 00:03:06.634
One thing in the clip,
we didn't fully see it,

00:03:06.634 --> 00:03:08.467
but I know you got
a scooter in this.

00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:10.000
You got to ride
a scooter around.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:11.434
Now, I would...

00:03:11.434 --> 00:03:13.934
I would not --
I'd be worried about that

00:03:13.934 --> 00:03:15.901
if I, as a performer,
had to get on a scooter.

00:03:15.901 --> 00:03:19.601
Are you somebody who's good
at those sort of things?

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:20.734
-I've been on a paddle board.

00:03:20.734 --> 00:03:21.734
If you've ever been
on a paddle board?

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:23.334
-I have. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:23.334 --> 00:03:25.033
-Didn't go great, you know?

00:03:25.033 --> 00:03:27.267
And the scooter was okay.

00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:28.467
I don't have the center of a --

00:03:28.467 --> 00:03:30.701
center of balance right
for a scooter,

00:03:30.701 --> 00:03:33.100
but after a while,
just the gravity does it alone.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.767
It's kind of --
It's hard to fall off.

00:03:34.767 --> 00:03:36.667
Just like,
as if you put a boulder

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:38.267
on a scooter and pushed it down.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:38.267 --> 00:03:41.100
But, yeah,
I get some moves out.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.701
It's -- It's very...
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:42.701 --> 00:03:45.133
-It's very, very sexy.
-Oh, wow.

00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:49.100
-Yeah. I want people
to be prepared for that.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.434
-Do you think it's sexy
for everyone,

00:03:50.434 --> 00:03:52.400
or do you think you just have
the right kind of body

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.934
to be sexy on a scooter?
-I think that -- yes.

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:58.167
Because I suppose
it's the -- it's the contrast

00:03:58.167 --> 00:04:01.300
with the kind
of the narrowness of...

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.400
That was a mistake.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.567
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.567 --> 00:04:06.534
I'm going to regret that
down the road.

00:04:06.534 --> 00:04:07.801
-Eh, we're gonna...

00:04:07.801 --> 00:04:10.300
We could edit it out
or leave it in, I feel like.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:11.567
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.567 --> 00:04:13.033
-But, yeah, I got
to do some scootering.

00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:14.801
I got to do some dancing,
which is fun.

00:04:14.801 --> 00:04:17.067
I get out all my skills, use six
of my faces.

00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:18.601
It's a whole thing.
-Did you --

00:04:18.601 --> 00:04:21.267
When you were a kid,
did you do any of

00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:23.367
the sort of fortune teller stuff
like Magic Eight Ball

00:04:23.367 --> 00:04:25.367
or Ouija board or any of those?
-We did some Ouija boards.

00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:27.100
And this is kind of like that.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.534
It's kind of like a Zoltar type
of machine, and then it can...

00:04:29.534 --> 00:04:31.434
But I don't know
if I would do it now.

00:04:31.434 --> 00:04:34.501
I feel a bit untethered now
that I would be like...

00:04:34.501 --> 00:04:37.300
For example, it was my kid's
birthday a couple of weeks ago

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:38.234
and we got a magician.

00:04:38.234 --> 00:04:39.834
He's mad into magic.

00:04:39.834 --> 00:04:43.033
And the magician said
that his wife was a palm reader

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:44.667
and she could come
and do the adults

00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:46.434
while everybody else was...

00:04:46.434 --> 00:04:48.234
And so she came.
Her name was Miss Zelda.

00:04:48.234 --> 00:04:49.634
I don't know if
it's her real name.

00:04:49.634 --> 00:04:52.868
-Yeah. And it seems pretty
fortuitous if that's it.

00:04:52.868 --> 00:04:54.534
-Maybe that's one
of those things

00:04:54.534 --> 00:04:55.968
where it's a self-fulfilling
thing.

00:04:55.968 --> 00:04:58.133
-Right. Like, if you're --
if you're a Zelda and you go --

00:04:58.133 --> 00:04:59.334
-She's not going to work in
insurance.

00:04:59.334 --> 00:05:01.200
-Right, exactly.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.334
-But anyway,
I didn't go to the lady

00:05:03.334 --> 00:05:04.801
because I felt like I don't know

00:05:04.801 --> 00:05:06.801
if I could deal with the --
maybe she's right.

00:05:06.801 --> 00:05:08.067
I don't want her to be right.

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:09.667
I just don't want
anything useful to happen.

00:05:09.667 --> 00:05:12.033
-I -- Yeah. I don't want
to know the future.

00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:14.000
-Right.
-Yeah. Mostly because

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.467
I feel like, good or bad,
if I get the information,

00:05:16.467 --> 00:05:18.267
I would somehow screw it up.

00:05:18.267 --> 00:05:20.200
-That's what I would do with
this machine where it's like,

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:23.033
I think I do it just because
everybody was doing it.

00:05:23.033 --> 00:05:24.200
But I wouldn't want
anybody to know

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:25.734
what was on my card
because I don't

00:05:25.734 --> 00:05:27.601
want next week for some [bleep]
to be like,

00:05:27.601 --> 00:05:28.968
"Are you an astronaut yet?"

00:05:28.968 --> 00:05:32.267
No, I'm not. I'm not.
I have napped.

00:05:32.267 --> 00:05:34.067
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:36.334
-You play a high school teacher,
as you mentioned.

00:05:36.334 --> 00:05:38.167
Do you think that's something
that would fit your skill set?

00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:40.601
Would you have liked that
as a path?

00:05:40.601 --> 00:05:41.834
-I think it's an incredible job.

00:05:41.834 --> 00:05:42.968
I don't know
if I have the patience for it.

00:05:42.968 --> 00:05:45.334
My sister is a teacher,
but I don't...

00:05:45.334 --> 00:05:47.467
I could --
I could be a professor.

00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:50.033
-Oh, interesting. What is
your distinction of the two?

00:05:50.033 --> 00:05:52.434
-Well, that's step two
that we haven't got to yet.

00:05:52.434 --> 00:05:55.033
But if there was anything
that I had some kind of in-depth

00:05:55.033 --> 00:05:56.534
knowledge in, it would be --
it would be...

00:05:56.534 --> 00:05:58.634
I don't know if I could deal
with kids all the time.

00:05:58.634 --> 00:06:00.334
-Yeah.
-They're so stupid.

00:06:00.334 --> 00:06:01.934
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.934 --> 00:06:03.534
By the way, I'm so...

00:06:03.534 --> 00:06:04.801
You did a wonderful show.

00:06:04.801 --> 00:06:06.067
The first time
you were on the show

00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:07.400
was for this wonderful show
called "Moone Boy."

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.133
-Yes.
-And I remember you were here,

00:06:09.133 --> 00:06:10.434
and that was like 2014.

00:06:10.434 --> 00:06:11.801
And I always remember
one of the things you said,

00:06:11.801 --> 00:06:13.434
this was before I even had kids,
you said, you know,

00:06:13.434 --> 00:06:14.667
the thing about kids
is they're so stupid.

00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:16.634
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:16.634 --> 00:06:18.267
-And now I got them
and you got them.

00:06:18.267 --> 00:06:19.667
And I want you to know,
I'm saying this

00:06:19.667 --> 00:06:22.100
with all authenticity,
I think about you all the time.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:23.634
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:23.634 --> 00:06:25.534
-Like when I --
Because I know it's you, too.

00:06:25.534 --> 00:06:26.868
Because when my kids
are misbehaving,

00:06:26.868 --> 00:06:28.701
I think in my head,
"You're so stupid."

00:06:28.701 --> 00:06:31.667
And it's in an Irish accent.
-Good.

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:33.100
I got to tell you,
a quick kid story

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:34.467
because this happened
a couple of weeks ago.

00:06:34.467 --> 00:06:35.834
So, I was -- I was -- I think

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:37.868
I was making breakfast
or something,

00:06:37.868 --> 00:06:40.100
and there's like a toilet
somewhere downstairs.

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:41.300
And my kid was on --

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:43.567
my six-year-old
was sitting on the toilet.

00:06:43.567 --> 00:06:46.067
And so we just have
a conversation.

00:06:46.067 --> 00:06:48.000
And so he's just, "Daddy!

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.000
What if people
didn't have phones?"

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.434
And I was like, "Well, you know,

00:06:55.434 --> 00:06:57.834
people didn't
always have phones."

00:06:57.834 --> 00:06:59.601
And he said, "What?!"

00:06:59.601 --> 00:07:02.667
And I said --
We're in different rooms.

00:07:02.667 --> 00:07:04.267
And I said,
"Well, when Daddy was young,

00:07:04.267 --> 00:07:06.434
we didn't have
any mobile phones."

00:07:06.434 --> 00:07:08.400
And then there was
this long pause.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.534
And then he said,
"I said bones!"

00:07:11.534 --> 00:07:16.934
[ Laughter ]

00:07:16.934 --> 00:07:18.367
And what I realized, though,

00:07:18.367 --> 00:07:21.000
there was -- there was
a good eight seconds there

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.400
where he thought
when I was young there was...

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:28.734
[ Laughter ]

00:07:28.734 --> 00:07:30.634
-I also like...

00:07:30.634 --> 00:07:33.167
-Like jellyfish people.
-Yeah. You had --

00:07:33.167 --> 00:07:35.667
You had sitting bones,
but no mobile bones.

00:07:35.667 --> 00:07:37.801
-[ Laughs ]
-You could stay in one place

00:07:37.801 --> 00:07:39.400
with your bones,
but they wouldn't go anywhere.

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:42.667
-Half man, half chair.
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:42.667 --> 00:07:44.100
What a delight
to have you back here.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:45.300
Thanks so much for joining us.
-Thank you.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.434
-So great to see you.
-Chris O'Dowd, everybody.

00:07:47.434 --> 00:07:49.868
"The Big Door Prize" premieres
Wednesday on Apple TV+.

00:07:49.868 --> 00:07:51.300
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

